// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row fnparam_struct_ref_mut_field_binds_byvalue (tier 3). A
// `ref mut` field binder in a FUNCTION-PARAMETER struct pattern —
// `fn probe(P { ref mut x, y }: P)` — is legal Rust: `x` binds `&mut i64` at the
// field's place, so `*x = *x + 1` is a write through a mutable reference. TODAY
// it is REFUSED BY NAME:
//     parameter pattern '#0': field 'x': `ref` / `ref mut` binding modes are
//     not supported at a function parameter — take the parameter by reference
//     instead
// ⚠ THE SENTENCE CHANGED 2026-09-09c AND THE DEFECT DID NOT. Before that commit
// the parameter door read only the field NAME and the optional rename, never
// IS_REF, so `x` was bound BY VALUE at the field type and the body was refused
// with two sentences that were both about a binding it did not have:
//     write through raw pointer requires unsafe context
//     deref-write: '=' left side must be a pointer or mutable reference
// The recursive binder walk that closed fnparam_array_pattern_binds_nothing and
// fnparam_tuple_nested_sub_binds_nothing has to DECIDE what a `ref` field means
// at a parameter, and it refuses rather than silently binding the wrong thing —
// which is the very class those two rows were. What `ref mut` wants is a
// REFERENCE TO THE PLACE (`lir_mirror_emit_pat_ref_bind`, a pattern-level node
// that does not exist at a body prologue), not the projection READ the walk
// emits, so it is a different mechanism and stays open: a MEASURED separating
// pair, not an assumption. The same `ref mut` field binder in a `let`, a
// `match` arm and an `if let` carries its mode
// (`pat.binding.explicit-ref-mut`), so this is the parameter door, not the
// modifier. `ref` (shared) at the same position is the same gap.
// MEASURED 2026-09-07v identically on the base compiler e045ad449 and on the
// `mut`-modifier landing — the modifier fix is orthogonal, `pat_byval_mut`
// excludes IS_REF by construction. Rust: exit 0.
package fnparam_struct_ref_mut_field_binds_byvalue;
struct P { x: i64, y: i64 }
fn probe(P { ref mut x, y }: P) -> i32 {
    *x = *x + 1i64;
    if *x + y != 8i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let p: P = P { x: 3i64, y: 4i64 };
    return probe(p);
}
